FILE: examples/spa/index.html, examples/spa/main.js, examples/spa/README.md
FEATURE: spa-example-serving
DATE: 2026-08-26
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'example-redesign'

Reported by the user with a screenshot: the SPA page rendered as an unstyled white page
with serif text, default buttons and an empty content area, while the other three example
pages looked as intended.

DIAGNOSIS
The page had been served from the repository root with `python3 -m http.server 8000` — the
command the other three examples use, and the one printed in their READMEs — and opened at
/examples/spa/. Under that server every root-absolute path in the page 404s. Confirmed by
request:

    /examples/spa/                200
    /shared/example.css           404
    /main.js                      404
    /lib/vUX/vUX-spaEngine.js     404

So main.js never ran (hence the empty #contentBoundary and no progress bar) and
example.css never loaded (hence serif text and unstyled buttons). Only the page's own
inline <style> block applied, which is why the top bar alone looked dark.

This is a defect in the example, not merely a wrong command: the page gave no indication
of what was wrong. It rendered as a broken page rather than as a page that could say why.

================================================================================
CHANGE 1 — Link the stylesheet by a path that resolves under both servers
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/shared/example.css">

NEW_SECTION
<!-- relative on purpose: resolves to /examples/shared/ under a repo-root static
     server, and to /shared/ under this example's own dev server (any route depth) -->
<link rel="stylesheet" href="../shared/example.css">

REASON
So the misconfigured case is legible rather than raw. Checked against the URL resolution a
browser actually performs, at every route depth:

    /examples/spa/   -> /examples/shared/example.css   (200 under a repo-root server)
    /examples/spa/index.html -> /examples/shared/example.css
    /            -> /shared/example.css                (200 under the dev server)
    /user/7      -> /shared/example.css
    /table       -> /shared/example.css

'..' cannot climb above the document root, which is what makes the same relative path
correct in both layouts. The script tag and data-library-root stay absolute: no single
path can serve both, because '/user/7' must resolve main.js from a different depth than
'/', which is exactly what root-absolute paths are for.

================================================================================
CHANGE 2 — Say so when the engine cannot load
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
(nothing — the page failed silently)

NEW_SECTION
<div id="bootNotice" class="boot-notice"> [...] This example needs its own dev server.
[...] cd examples/spa / python3 server.py [...] This notice disappears once the engine
boots. </div>

and in main.js, at the top of run():

    // main.js is running, so the engine can load: the served-wrong notice is not for us
    var notice = document.querySelector("#bootNotice");
    if (notice != null) notice.remove();

REASON
The notice is in the markup and therefore visible by default; it is removed by the only
code that can only run when the page was served correctly. That inverts the failure: the
page now explains itself precisely when it cannot work, and says nothing when it can. It
needs no detection logic, because main.js executing IS the detection.

================================================================================
CHANGE 3 — Give the page a hero that demonstrates the component
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
<!-- SPA content is swapped in here -->
<section id="contentBoundary"></section>

NEW_SECTION
<header class="hero">
    [...] <h1>Routing without the reload.</h1> [...]
    <div class="urlbar">
        <span class="urlbar-dot"></span>
        <span class="urlbar-path" id="urlPath">/</span>
        <span class="urlbar-meta">document loads: <b id="loadCount">–</b></span>
    </div>
</header>

<!-- SPA content is swapped in here -->
<section id="contentBoundary"></section>

REASON
Independent of the serving bug, this page was the one that least met "lead with the
component": it opened with a nav bar and an empty content area, where the other three open
with the component visibly working.

A SPA's claim is invisible by nature — the interesting thing is what does NOT happen. The
mock address bar makes it visible: the pathname updates live from markNav(), beside a
counter of real document loads held in sessionStorage. Navigating changes the path while
the counter stays put, which is the claim, demonstrated rather than asserted. Reloading
bumps it, on purpose, so the counter is legible as a measure rather than a decoration.
sessionStorage access is wrapped, since it throws outright in some privacy modes; the
counter then reads "n/a" and nothing else is affected.

================================================================================
CHANGE 4 — README leads with how this example differs
================================================================================

REASON
The "Run" section now opens with a blockquote stating that this example, unlike the other
three, does not run under a repository-root static server, and explains the two things a
plain static server cannot provide (a history fallback, and the library at the declared
root-absolute path). Previously the correct command was present but unremarkable, and a
reader moving between examples had no reason to expect a different one.

================================================================================
CHANGE 5 — Route fragments were not aligned with the rest of the page
================================================================================

Reported by the user with a screenshot: injected route content sat flush against the
left edge of the viewport while every other block was inset.

OLD_SECTION
<!-- SPA content is swapped in here -->
<section id="contentBoundary"></section>

    #contentBoundary{min-height:340px; padding:44px 0 12px}

NEW_SECTION
<section class="route">
    <div class="wrap">
        <!-- SPA content is swapped in here -->
        <div id="contentBoundary"></div>
    </div>
</section>

    .route{padding:44px 0 16px}
    #contentBoundary{min-height:180px}

REASON
#contentBoundary was the only layout block on the page whose content did not pass through
.wrap, the class that carries the 1080px max-width and the 24px gutter. Every other block
nests its content in one; this one was itself the section, and fragments are injected
directly as its children, so nothing constrained them.

Fixed structurally rather than by giving #contentBoundary its own max-width and auto
margins: the section must stay full-width so its bottom rule spans the viewport like the
others, so the section and the constrained box have to be different elements. The engine
only ever replaces #contentBoundary's children, so adding ancestors above it changes
nothing for the component.

min-height drops from 340px to 180px at the same time. Its purpose is to stop the shell
collapsing between routes; 340px was enough to leave a visible void under a two-line
fragment, which is what made the misalignment so conspicuous in the first place.

An audit of the other three example pages found no equivalent case — every section,
header and footer in them routes its content through .wrap.

================================================================================
CHANGE 6 — Table header stuck 62px down inside its own scroll box
================================================================================

OLD_SECTION
    #stockTable th{ [...] position:sticky; top:62px; z-index:2; }

NEW_SECTION
    #stockTable th{ [...]
        /* sticky resolves against .table-scroll, the scrolling ancestor — not the
           viewport — so this is 0, not the height of the top bar */
        position:sticky; top:0; z-index:2; }

REASON
top:62px was chosen to clear the sticky top bar, which would be right if the header stuck
to the viewport. It does not: .table-scroll is an overflow:auto ancestor, so it is the
scrolling box the sticky header resolves against, and the offset was pushing the header
62px down inside that box instead of pinning it to the top of the table.

VERIFICATION
Both servers run. Under the repo-root server /examples/shared/example.css returns 200;
under the dev server /shared/example.css returns 200 and the boot notice is present in the
served HTML. Browser-equivalent URL resolution of the relative stylesheet path checked at
five base URLs covering both layouts and all route depths. main.js re-checked for syntax.
The page itself was not opened in a browser — there is none in this environment.
